= BITwiki Constitutional Index: Canonical Unified Edition =

== Document Purpose ==

This document serves as the '''canonical unified index''' for BITwiki's constitutional framework. It merges content from multiple version lineages:

'' '''v1.0.0 Framework''' (Constitutional_index.md, Constitutional_index1.md): Philosophical foundations, epistemic framework, research integration
'' '''v2.0.0 Index''' (Constitutional_index2-5.md): Constitutional hierarchy, amendment process, SMW implementation
'' '''v3.0.0 Index''' (Constitutional_index6.md): Streamlined structure with core principles focus
'' '''v2.established''' (Constitutional_index7.md): SMW property refinements

== Section I: Constitutional Documents (v2.0.0/v3.0.0 Structure) ==

BITwiki's constitutional framework consists of three interconnected documents:

=== BITwiki Charter ===

'''Status:''' Foundational declaration
'''Scope:''' Mission, values, core commitments
'''Location:''' [[BITwiki Charter]]

The Charter declares our purpose. It establishes what BITwiki is—a recursive intelligence field for systems science knowledge. It articulates our core commitments: radical epistemic humility, radical skepticism, hyper-realism, nullification of non-signals, scoped recursion, ontological transparency.

Read this first if you want to understand why BITwiki exists and what principles guide it.

=== BITwiki Constitution ===

'''Status:''' Structural framework
'''Scope:''' Organizational structure, processes, epistemic framework
'''Location:''' [[BITwiki Constitution]]

The Constitution establishes the architectural framework. It defines our ontological approach—flexible types, not rigid categories. It articulates our epistemic framework—graduated markers, not numerical scores. It describes our recursive architecture and field coherence.

Read this if you want to understand how BITwiki is structured and how it operates.

=== BITwiki Foundational Triad ===

'''Status:''' Conceptual foundation
'''Scope:''' Core concepts and their interrelationships
'''Location:''' [[BITwiki Foundational Triad]]

The Triad defines the three foundational concepts: Axiology (value), Semantics (meaning), Axology (action). It explains how they interpenetrate and how they generate derivative principles.

Read this if you want to understand the conceptual underpinnings of BITwiki approach.

=== BITwiki Evidence and Epistemics ===

'''Status:''' Operational protocol
'''Scope:''' How we evaluate evidence and mark confidence
'''Location:''' [[BITwiki Evidence and Epistemics]]

This document defines how BITwiki records evidence quality, epistemic status, and confidence rationale. It establishes our approach to source evaluation and claim structure.

Read this if you want to understand how we know what we claim to know.

== Section II: Core Principles (v3.0.0 Enhanced) ==

=== Radical Epistemic Humility ===

All knowledge is provisional. We mark confidence not to display certainty but to calibrate doubt. Every claim carries uncertainty. Every model simplifies. Every framework has boundaries.

This is not modesty. It is precision. A claim with explicit uncertainty is more valuable than one with false confidence.

=== Radical Skepticism ===

We apply systematic doubt to all claims, including our own. No immunity. No authority beyond question. We doubt the instruments of our doubt.

This is not cynicism. It is rigor. Beliefs strengthened through trial are stronger than those protected from test.

=== Hyper-Realism ===

We maintain fidelity to system dynamics over representational accuracy. Models are evaluated by predictive power, not pictorial fidelity. Truth is operational coherence.

This is not relativism. Some models work better than others. But the criterion is performance, not correspondence to inaccessible reality.

=== Nullification of Non-Signals ===

We reject noise, fluff, filler, corporate language. Signal preservation requires noise suppression. Every word earns its place.

This is not minimalism for its own sake. It is respect for attention. Readers have limited time. We do not waste it.

=== Ontological Transparency ===

We make structure explicit and machine-readable. Entity types, relationships, epistemic status—all declared, all queryable.

This is not bureaucracy. It is enabling infrastructure. When structure is explicit, machines can navigate it. When machines can navigate it, humans can query in powerful ways.

